@page "/docs/get-started/by-hand"

@* What izzy init does in a Blazor Web App, step by step. Each step was checked against dotnet new blazor --empty
   on .NET 10, with and without a .Client project: the app builds, serves the stylesheet and posts the first page's
   form. *@

<ArticlePage Section="get-started" Slug="by-hand">
    <Lead>
        To set up IzzyBlazor in a Blazor Web App without <Code>izzy init</Code>, follow the same steps by hand. You install
        one package, link a stylesheet, add the theme and add a line to <Code>Program.cs</Code>. A <Code>.Client</Code>
        project gets its own package and registration.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Install the package</Heading>
        <TextBlock>Add the package to the server project:</TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">dotnet add package IzzyBlazor --prerelease</Code>

        <Heading Level="2">Add the stylesheet and the theme</Heading>
        <TextBlock>Link the stylesheet before your own, and put <Code>IzzyTheme</Code> in the <Code>head</Code>:</TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Components/App.razor" HighlightLines="6,9">@AppHead</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/izzy-theme">IzzyTheme</Anchor> sets the preset, brand color and fonts, as in
            <Code>&lt;IzzyTheme Preset="ThemePreset.Compact" Brand="#0a7d5a" /&gt;</Code>. It renders on the server, so the
            theme applies on first paint, even with JavaScript off. You don't add a script tag, because Blazor loads
            IzzyBlazor's script itself.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Import the components</Heading>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Components/_Imports.razor">@@using IzzyBlazor</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Use this instead of a <Code>global using</Code>. It brings the components into <Code>.razor</Code> files only, so
            names such as <Code>Table</Code> and <Code>Column</Code> don't clash with EF Core's attributes in your entity
            classes.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Register the services</Heading>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="Program.cs" HighlightLines="2">@ProgramCs</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>AddIzzyBlazor</Code> adds the toast and dialog services, and keeps toasts across a redirect. It also lets
            components read their cookies on the server, such as the cookie that stores the color scheme the user picked.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Add toasts and dialogs</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            To show toasts, put one <Anchor Href="docs/components/toast-region">ToastRegion</Anchor> in your layout. It
            works on static pages too:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Components/Layout/MainLayout.razor" HighlightLines="5">@Layout</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            On static pages, the ToastRegion also reads <Code>Announcer</Code>'s messages to screen readers (see
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/live-region">LiveRegion</Anchor>). In a layout without toasts, add an
            <Code>&lt;AnnouncerRegion /&gt;</Code> instead.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            To open dialogs from C# with <Code>DialogService</Code>, put one <Code>&lt;DialogHost /&gt;</Code> on the
            interactive page that opens them. Or put it in the layout and give the host a render mode:
            <Code>&lt;DialogHost @@rendermode="InteractiveServer" /&gt;</Code>. Dialogs written in the page's markup don't
            need a host. See <Anchor Href="docs/components/dialog">Dialog</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">The .Client project</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            With WebAssembly or Auto, components that run in the browser are in a <Code>.Client</Code> project. That project
            can't use ASP.NET Core's server framework, so it gets <Code>IzzyBlazor.Core</Code>. The server project keeps
            <Code>IzzyBlazor</Code>:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">dotnet add MyApp.Client package IzzyBlazor.Core --prerelease</Code>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="MyApp.Client/_Imports.razor">@@using IzzyBlazor</Code>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="MyApp.Client/Program.cs" HighlightLines="4">@ClientProgram</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            An Auto page renders on the server first and in the browser later, so it uses both registrations.
            <Code>AddIzzyBlazor</Code> on the server includes everything <Code>AddIzzyBlazorCore</Code> adds.
            <Code>App.razor</Code> always renders on the server, so you set up the stylesheet and theme only once, in the
            server project.
        </TextBlock>
        <Table Items="Packages" Caption="Packages" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params site-params-stack" Bordered="false" SmallScreen="TableSmallScreen.Stack" StackBelow="Length.Rem(40)">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Package" Width="Length.Rem(10)"><code>@context!.Name</code></TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Contains">@context!.Contains</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Install in">@context!.Where</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            Every component is in <Code>IzzyBlazor.Core</Code>. Both packages use the <Code>IzzyBlazor</Code> namespace, so
            the same markup works in either project. Keep both packages on the same version.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">From the full template</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            If you created the app without <Code>--empty</Code>, the full template's styles win over IzzyBlazor's until
            Bootstrap is in a layer. Its <Code>NavMenu</Code> also clashes with IzzyBlazor's. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/get-started/existing-app#keep-bootstrap">Keep Bootstrap</Anchor> and
            <Anchor Href="docs/get-started/existing-app#name-clashes">Name clashes</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Build a first page</Heading>
        <TextBlock>A form shows what static rendering does on its own:</TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Components/Pages/Signup.razor">@Signup</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Run the app and open <Code>/signup</Code>. The form posts, validates and shows its message without a circuit,
            even with JavaScript off. To run the same markup over a circuit, add <Code>@@rendermode InteractiveServer</Code>
            under <Code>@@page</Code>. See <Anchor Href="docs/components/form">Form</Anchor> for more.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/app-shell">AppShell</Anchor>: build your layout's header, sidebar and main area.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">Render modes</Anchor>: what components do on static and interactive pages.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/style-props">Style props</Anchor>: set spacing, sizes and colors on any component.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private sealed record Package(string Name, string Contains, string Where);

    private static readonly Package[] Packages =
    [
        new("IzzyBlazor", "Everything in IzzyBlazor.Core, plus the server code that reads and writes the request's cookies", "The server project"),
        new("IzzyBlazor.Core", "Every component, the CSS and IzzyBlazor's script, with no dependency on the ASP.NET Core server", "The .Client project, or a Razor class library"),
    ];

    private const string AppHead = """
        <head>
            <meta charset="utf-8" />
            <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
            <base href="/" />
            <ResourcePreloader />
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["_content/IzzyBlazor.Core/izzyblazor.css"]" />
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["app.css"]" />
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["MyApp.styles.css"]" />
            <IzzyTheme />
            <ImportMap />
            <HeadOutlet />
        </head>
        """;

    private const string ProgramCs = """
        var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
        builder.Services.AddIzzyBlazor();
        builder.Services.AddRazorComponents()
            .AddInteractiveServerComponents();

        var app = builder.Build();
        // ...
        app.MapRazorComponents<App>()
            .AddInteractiveServerRenderMode();

        app.Run();
        """;

    private const string ClientProgram = """
        using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly.Hosting;

        var builder = WebAssemblyHostBuilder.CreateDefault(args);
        builder.Services.AddIzzyBlazorCore();

        await builder.Build().RunAsync();
        """;

    private const string Layout = """
        @inherits LayoutComponentBase

        @Body

        <ToastRegion />
        """;

    private const string Signup = """
        @page "/signup"
        @using System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations

        <Container Size="ContainerSize.Sm" PaddingY="8">
            <Form Model="Model" FormName="signup" OnValidSubmit="Save">
                <TextField @bind-Value="Model!.Email" AutoComplete="email" />
                <Button Submit>Sign up</Button>
            </Form>
            @if (_signedUp)
            {
                <Alert Status="Status.Success">Thanks! We'll write to @Model!.Email.</Alert>
            }
        </Container>

        @code {
            [SupplyParameterFromForm(FormName = "signup")]
            private SignupModel? Model { get; set; }

            private bool _signedUp;

            protected override void OnInitialized() => Model ??= new();

            private void Save() => _signedUp = true;

            public sealed class SignupModel
            {
                [Required, EmailAddress]
                public string? Email { get; set; }
            }
        }
        """;
}
